**Read:** [Sedimentary Geology — John Southard, MIT 12.110](../sources/MIT-Sedimentary-Geology.lingua#r_7FWZZ66CT5G1FP2JPQMWRV1YHM), the complete sediment composition, production/transport, and source context; [Introduction to Geochronology — Pieter Vermeesch](../../geology/sources/Introduction-to-Geochronology-Vermeesch.lingua#r_30AEAXYK3VV89FDX1TF9ZKJ84J), the full detrital-geochronology treatment; [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the complete relevant mineral-survival/composition descriptions.

Sediment composition reflects source rocks plus weathering, erosion, transport, hydraulic sorting, storage, recycling, deposition, and later alteration. A mineral fraction can become concentrated because of density/size and survival rather than simply because it dominated the source.

Framework-grain counts, heavy minerals, lithic fragments, mineral chemistry, ages, and isotopic observations constrain different parts of the history. Sampling and analytical support matter: a detrital-grain age distribution is a selected observed population, not automatically a direct proportion of source-area rock volumes.

Recycled grains can preserve older source histories unrelated to the immediately eroded formation. A youngest detrital age can constrain deposition under specific assumptions but is not generally the exact depositional age. Inheritance, contamination, analytical uncertainty, and incomplete sampling require explicit treatment.

**Work:** reconstruct a provenance argument from a complete published or teaching case. Separate source, sorting/survival, recycling, and diagenesis as possible explanations for an observed composition. Independently check one proportion/age-bound claim and design a discriminating mineral-chemical, textural, or chronological observation. Explain which source history remains nonunique.

Read the complete sediment-composition and detrital-time treatments. The later sedimentary project requires a source-to-burial history supported by multiple evidence types rather than a tectonic label inferred from one compositional plot.

